","PETITIONERs are the heirs of a plaintiff who sued in 1995 at Biswanath in Sylhet for specific performance of a contract of 1969 over 7.66 acres, whose suit was decreed by the Assistant Judge in 1996 and then reversed on appeal in 2001. RESPONDENTs are the defendants' heirs. The heirs say the appellate court, as the last court of facts, failed to weigh the evidence, in particular the testimony of the attesting witnesses and the defendants' own admissions on the document.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,ALLOWED,"The court found that the plaintiff had examined three witnesses, including the attesting witnesses of the bainapatra, all of whom supported his case, and that the defendants had admitted their signatures and thumb impressions on the document of 1969. That being so, the trial judge had rightly held the bainapatra to be genuine and had rightly found that the later document of 1963 and the deed of 1975 were brought into being to defeat it. The appellate court had reversed those findings without properly weighing the evidence, so the rule was answered in the plaintiff's favour and the trial decree affirmed.",7597f1d007a938868c23e8b0df5c727f87bbe1aab263db73d10897535368673f
