The instant revision has been argued with great ability by the 
learned Advocates of both sides. As a result of their arguments on 
question of facts and law, the issue involved in the Rule has been 
narrowed down to a very short compass as to the admissibility of 
documentary evidence. 
The learned Assistant Judge, Mirsharai Sadar, Chattogram 
allowed the Other Suit No. 49 of 2005 on 28.03.2010. Decree was 

<<PAGE 2>>
2 
 
drawn on 01.04.2010. Other Appeal No. 97 of 2017 (former 201 of 
2010) was dismissed on 18.10.2018 by the Special District Judge, 
Chattogram. Challenging the same, the defendants filed the instant 
revision and obtained Rule on 08.04.2019.  
Plaintiff-opposite party Nos. 1, 3 and 4 entered appearance in 
the Rule.  
The plaint was amended two times before commencement of 
the trial. The plaintiffs filed the suit for declaration of title and 
confirmation of possession in 15 decimals of land recorded in R.S 
Khatian No. 3241, R.S Plot No. 5239 corresponding to B.S Khatian 
No. 1125, B.S Plot Nos. 5919 and 5920. The plaintiffs further prayed 
for declaration that the registered sale deed Nos. 2229 dated 
03.05.1960, 2508 dated 04.04.1987 and 116, 117 both dated 
11.02.2005 and Mutation Case No. 54/88-89 are illegal and not 
binding upon the plaintiffs. The plaintiffs also prayed for a permanent 
injunction.  
The plaintiffs’ case is that Abdul Kader was the owner of 22.5 
decimals of land recorded in R.S. Plot No. 5239. By a registered deed 
of gift No. 2329 dated 01.06.1946 (ext. 3), he gifted the said 22.5 
decimals of lands and other lands to his two daughters, namely 
Rowshan Banu and Hosne Banu in equal share. Rowshan Banu died 
leaving a daughter and a son who inherited her share in the lands. By 

<<PAGE 3>>
3 
 
a registered sale deed No. 3371 dated 27.04.1974 (ext. 5), the heirs of 
Rowshan Banu sold the suit land consisting of 15 decimals, which 
was recorded in R.S. Plot No. 5239, to Abul Hossain. Hosne Banu by 
a registered sale deed No. 3083 dated 11.06.1974 (ext. 4) sold lands 
including the land recorded in the R.S Khatian No. 5239 to Abul 
Hossain. Thus, Abul Hossain became the owner of the lands including 
the suit land. The plaintiffs purchased the suit land from Abul Hossain 
by a registered deed No. 1698 dated 07.05.1987 (ext. 7). Precisely, 
this is the chain of title of the plaintiffs.  
The contesting defendants admitted that Abdul Kader was the 
owner of 22.5 decimals of land. They denied the execution of the gift 
deed dated 01.06.1946 (ext. 3) by Abdul Kader and subsequent 
execution of the sale deed dated 11.06.1974 (ext. 4) and another sale 
deed dated 07.05.1987 (ext. 7). Their specific case is that Abdul 
Kader kept the said 22.5 decimals of land in mortgage to one Munshi 
Mia in 1956. Munshi Mia was in possession of the land till 1960 as 
mortgagee. Abdul Kader could not redeem the mortgage. He was in 
need of money. Thereafter, by a registered sale deed No. 2229 dated 
03.05.1960 (ext. Kha) Abdul Kader sold 15 
ଷ
ସ decimals of land out of 
22.5 decimals to Munshi Mia’s wife Ayesha Khatun. Thereafter, 
Ayesha Khatun sold the said 15 
ଷ
ସ decimals of land (suit land) to 
defendant Nos. 1 and 2 and to their brother Moazzem (since 

<<PAGE 4>>
4 
 
deceased). After purchase, they mutated the land in their names, vide 
Mutation Case No. 54/88-89. Thereafter, the defendants sold part of 
the suit land to other defendants.  
As noted earlier, the suit was decreed in favour of the plaintiff 
by the trial Court and the appellate Court below. Mr. Md. Omar 
Farook, the learned Advocate appearing for the defendant-petitioners, 
submits that the foundation of the chain of title of the plaintiffs is the 
registered gift deed dated 01.06.1946 (ext. 3). At the time of tendering 
the certified copy of the said gift deed in evidence by the PW-1 the 
defendants raised objection which is noted in the deposition sheet as 
well as on the back page of exhibit-3. As such, the gift deed (ext. 3) 
was inadmissible evidence as per provisions of the Evidence Act. 
However, both the Courts below did not consider this aspect of the 
case and decreed the suit treating exhibit-3 as admissible evidence.  
The next argument of Mr. Omar Farook is that the plaintiffs 
failed to prove their possession in the suit land. On question of 
possession, the trial Court relied on the oral evidence of the PWs and 
disbelieved the testimony of DWs and held that the plaintiffs are in 
possession of the suit land. The appellate Court below came to the 
same conclusion. Mr. Farook argued that both the Courts below did 
not at all consider the B.S. Khatian, rent receipts, mutation case 
proceedings tendered in evidence by the defendants in support of 

<<PAGE 5>>
5 
 
proof of possession of the suit land by them. The final argument of 
Mr. Farook is that the suit is barred by limitation. 
In reply, Mr. Sk. Md. Morshed, the learned Senior Advocate 
appearing for the plaintiff-opposite parties, submits that the 
defendants in their pleading did not deny the execution of the 
registered gift deed dated 01.06.1946 (ext. 3). As such, as per 
provision of Order VIII rule 5 of the Code of Civil Procedure (CPC) 
read with Section 58 and clause (b) of Section 65 of the Evidence Act, 
the certified copy of exhibit-3 is admissible evidence. On question of 
possession and limitation, Mr. Morshed submits that both the Courts 
below concurrently found that the plaintiffs are in possession of the 
suit land and that the suit is not barred by limitation.  
The point raised by Mr. Farook that exhibit-3, which is the 
certified copy of the registered gift deed dated 01.06.1946 tendered in 
evidence by PW-1, is inadmissible is taken up first.  
Exhibit-3 is the foundation of the plaintiffs’ case. It is noted 
that the defendants also raised objection as to admissibility of certified 
copies of sale deed dated 27.04.1974 (ext. 5) and sale deed dated 
07.05.1987 (ext. 7) in evidence which also form part of the chain of 
title of the plaintiffs. The objections are recorded in the deposition 
sheet as well as on the back page of the exhibited documents.  

<<PAGE 6>>
6 
 
The contents of documents may be proved either by primary or 
by secondary evidence (Section 61 of Evidence Act). Section 62 
provides that primary evidence means the document itself produced 
for the inspection of the Court. Under clause (1) of Section 63 
certified copies given under the provisions of Section 65 are included 
as secondary evidence. Section 64 states that documents must be 
proved by primary evidence except in the cases mentioned in other 
provisions of the Evidence Act. Cases in which secondary evidence 
relating to documents may be given are set out in Section 65. The 
relevant provisions of Section 65 are reproduced below, 
“65. Secondary evidence may be given of the existence, 
condition or contents of a document in the following cases:– 
(a) ….. 
(b) when the existence, condition or contents of the original 
have been proved to be admitted in writing by the person 
against whom it is proved or by his representative in 
interest;  
(c) ….. 
(d) …..  
(e) when the original is a public document within the 
meaning of section 74;  
(f) …..  
(g) ….. 
In cases (a), (c), and (d)…..  
In case (b), the written admission is admissible.  
In case (e) or (f), a certified copy of the document, but no 
other kind of secondary evidence, is admissible. 

<<PAGE 7>>
7 
 
In case (g)…..”. 
When a certified copy of a document is tendered in evidence 
the same is subject to a large number of limitations. In H. Siddiqui vs. 
A. Ramalingam, (2011) 4 SCC 240 it is held: 
“The provisions of Section 65 of the 1872 Act provide for 
permitting the parties to adduce secondary evidence. However, 
such a course is subject to a large number of limitations. In a 
case where the original documents are not produced at any 
time, nor has any factual foundation been laid for giving 
secondary evidence, it is not permissible for the court to allow 
a party to adduce secondary evidence. Thus, secondary 
evidence relating to the contents of a document is 
inadmissible, until the non-production of the original is 
accounted for, so as to bring it within one or other of the cases 
provided for in the section. The secondary evidence must be 
authenticated by foundational evidence that the alleged copy is 
in fact a true copy of the original. Mere admission of a 
document in evidence does not amount to its proof. Therefore, 
the documentary evidence is required to be proved in 
accordance with law. The court has an obligation to decide the 
question of admissibility of a document in secondary evidence 
before making endorsement thereon”. 
In the above-mentioned reported case, the photocopy of the 
power of attorney was shown to the respondent in his cross-
examination and he admitted his signature. The Indian Supreme Court 
held that the respondent had merely admitted his signature on the 
photocopy of the power of attorney and did not admit the contents 

<<PAGE 8>>
8 
 
thereof. Admissibility of a document or contents thereof may not 
necessarily lead to drawing any inference unless the contents thereof 
have some probative value.  
In Abani Mohan Sana vs Assistant Custodian Vested 
Property, Chandpur and others, 39 DLR (AD) 223 the plaintiff’s 
case was based on a registered gift deed. He tendered in evidence the 
certified copy of the said deed as exhibit-1. In cross-examination, the 
plaintiff deposed that the original gift deed was burnt. He admitted 
that his house was not burnt. P.W.2, who was the scribe, was the only 
other witness who stated that the executants signed the deed after it 
was written by him. The Apex Court observed that the evidence of 
P.W.2 cannot by itself prove the genuineness of the gift deed. It was 
held by majority (4:1): 
“In the present case the plaintiff is claiming his title to the 
suit land on the basis of a deed of gift. I have got no doubt in 
my mind that the initial onus was on him to prove his title. The 
observation made in 32 DLR (AD) 29 would be of no avail to 
the appellant, as he failed to discharge his initial onus by 
proving his document of title. Had he succeeded to do so then 
the onus would have shifted on the Enemy Property Authority 
to prove that the executants of the plaintiff's document, the 
sons of Surendra Mohan Saha, were residing in an enemy 
country at the relevant time”. 
Under Section 79 of the Evidence Act, a Court is bound to draw 
the presumption that a certified copy of a document is genuine and 

<<PAGE 9>>
9 
 
also that the concerned officer signed it in the official character which 
he claimed in the said document. But such a presumption is 
permissible only if the certified copy is substantially in the form and 
purported to be executed in the manner provided by law in that 
manner. In other words, if the certified copy is executed substantially 
in the form and in the manner provided by the law, the Court raises a 
rebuttable presumption in regard to its genuineness. The scope of 
Section 79 was considered in Bhinka and others vs. Charan Singh, 
AIR 1959 SC 960. In this case, three Courts rejected the certified copy 
of the record-of-rights on the ground that it was not genuine on the 
basis of not only the internal evidence furnished by the document but 
also on other evidence. In this case, neither the official diary of the 
Patwary, who was the authorised officer to issue certified copies of 
the record-of-rights, was produced before the Court to prove that the 
prescribed procedure was followed nor the extract noted in the diary 
to disclose that the Patwary made any note of payment as required by 
law. The Indian Supreme Court held that it cannot, therefore, be said 
that the certified copy was issued by the Patwary in substantial 
compliance with the provisions of law governing such issue. It 
follows that the Court is not bound to draw the presumption in regard 
to its genuineness. It was further held that even if there was any 
rebuttable presumption, it was so rebutted. 

<<PAGE 10>>
10 
 
In the instant case, the plaintiffs neither called the concerned 
officer who issued the certified copies of exhibit Nos. 3, 5 and 7 nor 
took steps to prove that those exhibits are substantially in the form and 
purported to be executed in the manner directed by law in that behalf. 
Without taking such steps, the Court is not bound to draw the 
presumption of genuineness of exhibit Nos. 3, 5 and 7 under Section 
79 of the Evidence Act when the other side raised objection as to their 
admissibility at the time of tendering those in evidence. 
In Sova Rani Guha alias Sova Rani Gupta vs. Abdul Awal 
Mia and others, 47 DLR (AD) 45, the certified copy of the kabala 
was exhibited without any objection. An explanation was given for 
non-production of the original kabala. It was held:  
“A registered sale deed is the primary evidence of the 
interest sold. Under Section 74(2) of the Evidence Act, 1882 
public record of a private document like a sale deed when kept 
in the office of the Sub-Registrar to which the public generally 
have access falls within the category of public document and a 
certified copy of such a document can be adduced as a 
secondary evidence, though the reception of such evidence 
will be of a weak character in place of the registered document 
evidencing the sale. 
Where a case for the reception of secondary evidence is 
made out under the provisions of Section 65, a certified copy 
of a registered document becomes admissible in proof of the 
existence, condition and contents of the original document 
under the provision of Section 57 (5) of the Registration Act, 

<<PAGE 11>>
11 
 
but the contents of the documents may not be conclusive 
evidence. 
The production of a certified copy of a sale deed does not, 
however, warrant the presumption of due execution of the 
original document. A party producing secondary evidence of a 
document is not relieved of the duty of proving the execution 
of the original. Even in a case where a document is exhibited 
without objection the Court is to be satisfied as to the 
execution of the document”. 
In Dr. Md. Forman Ali Miah vs. Nizamuddin, 12 LM (AD) 
138 no objection was raised at the time of proving exhibit-Kha by 
producing the certified copy. It was held that when the original is a 
public document under Section 74 of the Evidence Act and the 
original is a document of which a certified copy is permitted by the 
Act or by any other law in force in Bangladesh to be given in evidence 
the same may be given/filed in a Court and unless any objection is 
raised to such document when the same is filed it is very much 
admissible in evidence. 41 DLR (AD) 97 was followd and relied upon 
in Dr. Md. Forman Ali Miah. 
The above case laws unequivocally suggest that when a party to 
the proceedings desires to prove a document, he can do so by 
producing the certified copy of the original document by laying the 
factual foundation for giving secondary evidence as per provisions of 
Section 65. Complication arises when the other side raises objection 
as to admissibility of the certified copy in evidence. Once objection is 

<<PAGE 12>>
12 
 
raised the party adducing the secondary evidence has no option but to 
follow and satisfy the requirements of the Evidence Act for 
admissibility of such secondary evidence.  
Against the backdrop of the factual matrix of the instant case, 
the proposition of law can be looked into from a different procedural 
perspective. Order XIII, rule 3 of the CPC empowers the trial Court to 
reject any document at any stage of the suit which it considers 
irrelevant or otherwise inadmissible even after admission of the 
document (Kissen vs. Ram, 12 WR 13). When a document is marked 
as exhibit without objection, the admissibility of the same cannot be 
challenged at appellate stage or subsequent stage [44 DLR (AD) 162, 
12 LM (AD) 138, (2004) 7 SCC 107]. The rationale behind the 
principle is that objection as to the mode of proof falls within the 
procedural law. Therefore, such objections could be waived. The rule 
of fair play envisages that an objection, if taken at the appropriate 
point of time, would enable the party tendering the secondary 
evidence to cure the defect and resort to such mode of proof as would 
be regular. The omission to object becomes fatal because by his 
failure the party entitled to object allows the party tendering the 
secondary evidence to act on an assumption that the opposite party is 
not serious about the mode of proof [(2004) 7 SCC 107]. In the case 
in hand, in spite of objection raised by the defendants at the time of 
tendering exhibit Nos. 3, 5 and 7 in evidence by the plaintiffs, which 

<<PAGE 13>>
13 
 
are certified copies of gift deed and sale deeds forming essential part 
of the chain of title of plaintiffs, they did not take steps to prove those 
documents in accordance with the procedure prescribed by law. 
Failure of the plaintiffs to prove documents (ext. 3, 5 and 7) in 
accordance with law in the face of objection goes to the root of the 
matter regarding admissibility. Regrettably, the trial Court admitted 
those exhibits in evidence and passed the decree based on those in 
violation of the law. 
Mr. Morshed appearing for the plaintiff-opposite parties refers 
to exhibit-Ga series which are the attested copies of Union Parishad 
arbitration proceedings dated 11.01.1989 and exhibit-Gha and Gha (1) 
which are certified copies of Mutation Case No. 54/88-89 and 
mutation proposal case No. 23/88-89 giving rise to the mutation case 
and submits that transfer of the suit land by exhibit-3 had always been 
admitted by the defendants and as such exhibit-3 is not required to be 
proved under Section 58 (facts admitted need not be proved) and 
clause (b) of Section 65 of the Evidence Act. Exhibit-Ga series and 
exhibit-Gha and Gha (1) were tendered in evidence by the defendants. 
Exhibit-Ga series are the photocopies of handwritten documents 
attested by the Union Parishad Chairman pertaining to an award given 
in a local salish conducted by the local Union Parishad Chairman. The 
award has no legal force. The admissibility of exhibit-Ga series in 
evidence is highly questionable. In any event, it cannot be considered 

<<PAGE 14>>
14 
 
as admission of the defendants as to the deed gift dated 01.04.1946 
(ext. 3). The said gift deed has not been mentioned in exhibit-Gha 
(order dated 19.02.1989 passed by the Assistant Commissioner (Land) 
in Mutation Case No. 54/88-89). However, in exhibit-Gha(1) (report 
of the concerned Tahsildar dated 24.01.1989 in Mutation Proposal 
Case No. 23/88-89), the gift deed of 1946 (ext. 3) as well as the 
mortgage of the suit land in favour of Munshi Mia by Abdul Kader in 
1956 and subsequent sale of the same to Munshi Mia’s wife Ayesha 
Khatun by Abdul Kader which is the defendants’ case, were 
mentioned. It is stated in exhibit-Gha(1), “
predecessor-in-
interest of plaintiffs
”.  The statements made in exhibit- 
Gha(1) negates the contention of Mr. Morshed.  
Mr. Morshed argued that the defendants in their pleading did 
not categorically deny the plaintiffs’ chain of title which is essentially 
linked to exhibit-3, rather admitted in paragraph 20 of the written 
statement that 
and as such, exhibit-3 is admissible evidence.  
Mr. Farook, appearing for the defendant-petitioners, submits 
that the statement in question implies the alternative case of the 

<<PAGE 15>>
15 
 
defendants that if there be any title of the plaintiffs the same has been 
barred by limitation. Mr. Farook submits that the statement does not 
tantamount to admission of the plaintiffs’ case. Mr. Morshed, on the 
other hand, submits that the pleading of the defendants being evasive 
and not being specific in stating the real facts starting from paragraph 
18 of the written statements, the plaintiffs’ case is admitted by 
operation of law. In this regard, I note that the defendants in paragraph 
No. 11 of their written statement categorically denied the plaintiffs’ 
chain of title including the gift deed dated 01.06.1946 (ext. 3) by 
stating, “…
”.  
Order VIII, rule 5 of the CPC states that every allegation of fact 
in the plaint, if not denied specifically or by necessary implication, or 
stated to be not admitted in the pleading of the defendant, shall be 
taken to be admitted except as against a person under disability. The 
case of Zafela Begum and others vs. Atikulla and others, 16 BLC 
(AD) 46 = 4 XP (AD) 2 can be referred to as an example as to how 
evasive denial in written statement can be considered as admission of 
the plaintiff’s case. In paragraph 7 of the plaint of the reported case, 
the plaintiffs specifically stated that Money Execution Case Nos. 110 
and 111 of 1952 were disposed of on compromise and sale of .07 acre 

<<PAGE 16>>
16 
 
in plot No. 29 towards west was confirmed and the rest .57 acre was 
set aside, and the sale in respect of land in plot No. 27 was set aside. 
The defendants simply stated in paragraph 6(Kha) of their written 
statement that the sale was not set aside rather confirmed. The Apex 
Court held that the statements made in the written statement did not 
meet the positive claim of the plaintiffs on the point of setting aside 
the auction sale. It was further held that according to the law of 
pleadings, the defendant is bound to deal specifically each allegations 
of fact, the truth of which is not admitted. The written statement must 
not only deal with specifically, the defendant must also answer the 
point of substance. If his denial of fact is not specific but evasive, the 
said fact shall be taken to be admitted. In such event the admission 
itself being proof, no other proof is necessary. 
Zafela Begum was followed and applied in Divisional Estate 
Officer, Bangladesh Railway and others vs. Jashimuddin and 
others, 24 BLC (AD) 36 wherein it was held that the defendants 
having not denied in their pleading or in their oral evidence as to the 
plaintiffs’ chronology of devolution of title and transfer made in their 
favour as stated in the plaint, the same is treated as admission under 
Order VIII rule 5 of the CPC. In the case in hand, the defendants, in 
my view, have specifically denied the foundation of the chain of title 
of the plaintiffs in written statement and set out their own case. 

<<PAGE 17>>
17 
 
Therefore, I am unable to accept Mr. Morshed’s contention on point 
of admission of the plaintiffs’ case by the defendants in their pleading. 
The relevant facts may be recalled. The plaintiffs’ case is that 
Abdul Kader gifted 22.5 decimals of land to his two daughters on 
01.06.1946 (ext. 3). The defendant’s case is that Abdul Kader did not 
make such gift. He sold 15
ଷ
ସ decimals of land out of the said 22.5 
decimals to Ayesha Khatun by a registered sale deed dated 03.05.1960 
(ext. Kha). The defendants tendered the original sale deed dated 
03.05.1960 in evidence. The trial Court did not disbelieve the 
defendants’ chain of title based on exhibit-Kha. The Court observed 
that earlier transfer of the suit land made in 1946 (ext. 3) in favour of 
the predecessor-in-interest of the plaintiffs would prevail over the 
subsequent transfers made to the predecessor-in-interest of the 
defendants. The observation is based on the maxim “qui prior est 
tempore potior est” jure meaning “he who prior in time is better in 
law” incorporated in Section 48 of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882. 
I have already held that the certified copy of the gift deed dated 
01.04.1946 (ext. 3) tendered in evidence by the plaintiffs is not 
admissible. Therefore, the very foundation of the plaintiffs’ case is 
‘not proved’. The argument of Mr. Morshed to consider the probative 
value of exhibit-3 is rejected for the reason that the same is 
inadmissible evidence. Question of examining the probative value 
arises when a document is/or may be admissible which is not the 

<<PAGE 18>>
18 
 
scenario in the instant case. Since the plaintiffs’ case is not proved, I 
refrain from dwelling upon the question of possession and limitation 
argued by Mr. Omar Farook appearing for the defendants. 
The defendants have successfully made out a case that exhibit 
Nos. 3, 5 and 7 tendered in evidence by the plaintiffs are inadmissible. 
Both the Courts below committed error of law resulting in an error in 
decreeing the suit causing failure of justice in treating the gift deed 
dated 01.04.1946 (ext. 3) and subsequent sale deeds dated 27.04.1974 
and 07.05.1987 respectively (ext. 5 and 7) as admissible evidence 
which form a chain of title of the plaintiffs. Hence, the Rule succeeds. 
In the Result, the Rule is made absolute. The judgments and 
decrees passed by the Courts below are set aside. The suit is 
dismissed.  
Send down the L.C.R. 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
Arif, ABO 
","PETITIONERs are defendants in a suit of 2005 at Mirsharai in Chattogram for declaration of title and confirmation of possession, which was decreed in 2010 and whose appeal was turned down in 2018, both courts accepting a gift deed of 1946 and two later sale deeds as links in the plaintiffs' chain of title. RESPONDENTs are the plaintiffs. The defendants say those three documents were not admissible, being unregistered and produced only as certified copies, so that the case had no foundation.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,ALLOWED,"The court held that the certified copy of the gift deed of 1946 and the later sale deeds of 1974 and 1987 were inadmissible in evidence, so that the very foundation of the plaintiffs' claim remained unproved and the question of their probative value did not arise once admissibility had failed. The maxim that priority in time gives a better title, incorporated in section 48 of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882, could not assist a plaintiff whose own chain rested on documents the law excluded. Both courts below having erred in law, the suit came to an end with the claim unproved.",b0fc5a06d8df7d40d641aac861532a33e2cc028479e1da5d133f14885eadee90
