","PETITIONER is a defendant in a suit of 2007 at Sarail in Brahmanbaria, brought for specific performance of an agreement to sell 16 decimals of land, in which the trial court struck out the plaint outright and the appellate court then set that rejection aside in 2013, leaving the suit to proceed. RESPONDENTs are the plaintiffs. She says the agreement on which the suit rests was never registered, and that after the amendment of the law in 2004 no decree of specific performance can rest on an unregistered instrument.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,ALLOWED,"The court read section 54A of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882 together with the provisions of the Registration Act and the Specific Relief Act and found that where no time for execution and registration of the sale instrument is fixed, six months is deemed to be the time, and that an unregistered instrument cannot found a decree of specific performance. The instrument here was of 2007, after the change in the law. The appellate court had therefore erred in setting aside the rejection of the plaint, and its order was effaced by the rule being answered in the defendant's favour.",9e482e8d6ab7bc5deb00d675e519a0a52e02b993d7b3a2ff83301c138502083a
