","PETITIONER was convicted in 2017 under section 138 of the Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881 on a complaint by a non-governmental organisation which had lent him money, and sentenced to six months of simple imprisonment with a fine of Tk. 15,00,000, the amount of the cheque he had issued and which was returned for want of funds. RESPONDENTs are the state and the complainant, whose notice of August 2015 went unanswered. The appellant was not represented at the hearing of the appeal.","Other: Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881",REFUSED,"The court found from the record that the complainant's representative had deposed and exhibited the necessary documents, and that as the appellant had remained absent throughout, that evidence stood neither cross-examined nor displaced, nor was he examined under section 342 of the Code. The memo of appeal took no ground that touched the factual basis of the case. The trial court was therefore justified in finding him guilty, and a sentence confined to the amount of the cheque could not be called harsh. The appeal failed, the deposit going to the complainant.",232e4e304d0e7ac1c6c3b4e14043897e12e8948213119220eb6559d0d0da73e4
