","PETITIONERs are the heirs of a defendant in a partition suit of long standing at Nilphamari, in which the Additional District Judge in 2011 allotted 4.32 acres of the suit property to five of the defendants, on the footing that the plaintiffs had not proved their title or possession over two of the seven slots into which the property was divided. RESPONDENTs are the plaintiffs and other defendants. The heirs say the allocation rested on a misreading of the pleadings and of the depositions of the plaintiffs' own witnesses.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,ALLOWED,"The court found, going through the depositions of the plaintiffs' second to fourth witnesses, that their testimony did not support the case pleaded for them, and that once the defendants asserted their own title and possession over the slots in question, the onus lay on the plaintiffs to displace that assertion, which they had not done. It found no basis whatever for the allocation of a share to those five defendants. The appellate decree to that extent was therefore unsustainable and was effaced, and the rule was answered in the heirs' favour, the preservation order being recalled.",27cc6b1c304a2264edb35966082fe7c3fc5fe94d0235421aa362378eda945375
