","PETITIONER is a defendant in a suit of 1997 at Saidpur in Nilphamari for a permanent injunction, which the Senior Assistant Judge decreed in 2001 and whose decree the Subordinate Judge then effaced on appeal later that year. RESPONDENTs are the plaintiffs' heirs. The defendant says the plaintiff never proved the absolute possession on which a suit for a permanent injunction depends, and that the appellate court decided the question of possession in the plaintiff's favour without the evidence needed to support that conclusion.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court noted the divergence of authority on what a plaintiff in an injunction suit must show, and found that the plaintiff had not established absolute possession of the land such as would support the decree the trial judge passed; it was the trial judge who had gone wrong on that question. The appellate judge had come to a lawful conclusion on possession and had displaced the trial decree on a proper view of the material. No merit appearing in the rule, it was brought to an end, the interim stay was recalled, and the appellate decree was left standing.",6e5fa67956ed79461430b5c08b4b966ea36287ad9f6f5aef3ea93752a08cb20b
