","PETITIONER is a plaintiff who sued in 1980 at Habiganj for specific performance of a bainapatra of February 1976, by which the first defendant had agreed to sell 26 decimals for Tk. 5,000 and had taken Tk. 4,000 and delivered possession. The Senior Assistant Judge turned the suit down in 1997 and the Joint District Judge affirmed that in 2002. RESPONDENTs are the defendants. He says he had performed his part and that the second defendant, who later purchased the land, knew of his agreement from the outset.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court found that the plaintiff had neither pleaded nor proved the source of the second defendant's alleged knowledge of the bainapatra, nor the date or place at which that knowledge was acquired, and that in his own evidence he could not say the second defendant knew of the agreement before purchasing the land. Without proof that the later purchaser took with notice, the agreement could not bind him, and no decree for specific performance could be made against the land in his hands. Both courts below had assessed the evidence correctly, and the rule was brought to an end.",b42a7cdbeca29af4941a644d99bc1bf07b9d9760e174a1338ad024c1c360d6cc
