","PETITIONERs are defendants in a partition suit of 2004 at Madaripur, in which the plaintiffs sought a separate share of 52 decimals out of 1.44 acres of a khatian said to descend from a common ancestor. The Senior Assistant Judge turned the suit down in 2014 and the Additional District Judge then decreed it on appeal in 2021. RESPONDENTs are the plaintiffs. The defendants say the appellate court reversed the trial court without considering the record of rights and the deeds on which the shares depend.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,ALLOWED,"The court found that the appellate judge had gone through the record, had reversed the trial judge on grounds that could not be supported, and had in any event disposed of the matter in a way that left both sides worse off, since a fresh partition suit would cost them time and money that a retrial would not. It took the view that the ends of justice would be met by sending the case back so that the pleadings could be amended and further evidence led on the shares. The appellate decree was effaced on those terms, with costs of Tk. 20,000 payable to the petitioners.",88938eaae6195c56113c6ad8b75b0ad047755dbdb13fbbccfbb523add2d8ca7e
