","PETITIONERs are defendants in a suit of 2015 at Charfashion in Bhola, brought for a declaration that a deed of heba-bil-ewaz executed in 1990 in favour of two of the defendants was collusive, void and not binding on the plaintiffs. The Joint District and Sessions Judge decreed the suit in 2018 and the Additional District Judge effaced that decree on appeal in 2019, holding the suit barred. RESPONDENTs are the plaintiffs. The defendants say the appellate court rightly found the matter already settled by an earlier partition decree.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court found that the question whether that deed was valid had already been gone into and decided in an earlier partition suit, in which the present plaintiffs were parties, so that the matter was settled between them and could not be litigated over again by force of section 11 of the Code of Civil Procedure. The appellate judge was therefore right to hold the later suit barred, and no illegality appeared in his finding. The rule was brought to an end, with the observation that the parties could raise their contentions about the deed in the earlier appeal that was still pending.",caa90745d0eb2bfd16632455b7790827e74f87924e3c2ff92cc4e3c9a2bc8857
