This Rule was issued calling upon the opposite party Nos. 
1-6 to show cause as to why the order dated 07.05.2019 passed  
by the learned Joint District Judge, 4th Court, Dhaka in Title Suit 
No. 277 of 2019 rejecting the application under section 151 of the 
Code of Civil Procedure for ad-interim order of injunction should 
not be set-aside and/or such other or further order or orders 
passed as to this Court may seem fit and proper.  

<<PAGE 2>>
2 
 
It is found that by Rule issuing order dated 19.05.2019 a 
Division Bench of this Court directed to the learned Joint District 
Judge, 4th Court, Dhaka to hear and dispose of the application for 
temporary injunction strictly on merit by applying its independent 
and judicial mind within 30th June, 2019 without fail and without 
giving any adjournment to the parties.  
 
Mr. Abdul Jalil, the learned Advocate appearing for the 
plaintiff-petitioner submits that the application for temporary 
injunction has already been disposed of on 05.11.2024 and 
therefore, this Rule has become in-fractious.  
 
Mr. Mahbubur Rahman, the learned Advocate appearing for 
the defendant-opposite party No. 6, on the other hand, concedes 
the submissions of the learned advocate for the plaintiff-
petitioner.  
Since the substantive application for temporary injunction 
has already been disposed of by the learned Joint District Judge, 
4th Court, Dhaka before whom the case is pending as per direction 
of this court, the Rule has lost its force.  
The Rule is, therefore, discharged as being in-fractious.  
The learned trial judge is, however, directed to hear and 
dispose of the suit being Title Suit No. 277 of 2019 expeditiously 
in accordance with law.  
 
Let a copy of this judgment be communicated to the Courts 
concerned at once. 
Md. Mansur Alam, J: 
I agree. 
","PETITIONER is a finance company which sued in 2019 at Dhaka for a declaration and applied for an ad interim order of injunction, which the Joint District Judge turned down in May 2019. This court then directed that the application for temporary injunction be heard on its own merits by a fixed date. RESPONDENTs are the defendants. The question now is whether anything survives in the rule issued against the refusal, the substantive application having since been disposed of by the trial court.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court found that the application for temporary injunction had in the meantime been disposed of on the merits by the judge concerned, in compliance with the direction given when the rule was issued, and that the order which had been challenged had thus ceased to have any operative effect. Both sides conceded that this was so. Nothing remained for the court to decide, and the rule was accordingly closed as having lost its purpose, with a direction to the trial judge to dispose of the suit with expedition.",ca258e85b6e4f5a877cfa38d5f7ea42065fa31b26bfa09c28a19e1dcdb74c97c
