","PETITIONERs are defendants in a suit of 2010 at Nangalkot in Cumilla, in which the plaintiffs obtained a decree of perpetual injunction over the disputed land, and whose appeal against it was turned down by the Joint District Judge in 2017. RESPONDENTs are the plaintiffs. The defendants say the land is undivided joint property in which they hold a share, that no partition has taken place, and that no injunction can therefore run against them merely because the plaintiffs are in possession.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court found that although the parties are co-sharers in the disputed holding and the land has not been partitioned by metes and bounds, the plaintiffs had established their exclusive possession of the disputed portion, and a co-sharer in such possession is entitled to protect it by an injunction against the other co-sharers until a partition takes place. The defendants' remedy, the court observed, lies in a partition suit rather than in resisting the injunction. No substance appearing in the revision, the rule was brought to an end.",3b86dc83e95df1fe771ab08bfa2c7bf09040db5887f5614e3e157d0c0432c73f
